REGIONAL REGULATION OF SEMARANG CITY

NUMBER 7 OF 2023

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE MAYOR OF SEMARANG,

Considering:

a. that women are a national asset who play a very important role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations and need to receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights to be empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally and receive guarantees of protection from acts of violence against women;
b. that in order to provide a means for women's self-actualization and guarantee protection in the community in the city of Semarang, there is a need for a regional regulation governing the empowerment and protection of women;
c. that based on Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law, regional governments have authority in the administration of governmental affairs in the field of empowerment and protection of women;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to stipulate a Regional Regulation concerning Empowerment and Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia:
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions Within the Provinces of East Java, Central Java, West Java, and the Special Region of Yogyakarta (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 45);
3. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
4. Law Number 11 of 2023 concerning Central Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6867);

With Mutual Approval,

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SEMARANG CITY

and

THE MAYOR OF SEMARANG

HAVE DECIDED:

To Stipulate:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN.

Article 2

Empowerment and Protection of Women is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. non-discrimination;
c. gender equality and gender justice;
d. justice and legal certainty;
e. benefit;
f. participatory;
g. accountability; and
h. inclusion.

Article 3

Empowerment and Protection of Women aims to:
a. improve the quality of life of women;
b. increase women's participation in the development process;
c. encourage women's leadership and women's bargaining position in decision-making in various development processes, both planning, implementation and monitoring and evaluation;
d. increase the ability of women to manage the economy, both in meeting household needs and in opening productive and independent employment opportunities;
e. provide protection of women's rights from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment;
f. prevent all forms of Violence Against Women;
g. provide services and recovery for women victims of violence.
h. increase community participation in the Protection of Women;
i. realize a social life that is safe and free from all forms of Violence Against Women; and
j. increase the strengthening and development of institutions providing Women's Empowerment services.

Article 4

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. authority and responsibility of regional government;
b. women's rights;
c. Women's Empowerment;
d. Protection of Women;
e. strategy for Empowerment and Protection of Women;
f. mechanism for the implementation of Empowerment and Protection of Women;
g. institutions;
h. participation;
i. guidance and supervision; and
j. funding.

CHAPTER II

AUTHORITY AND RESPONSIBILITY

OF REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

Article 5

1. The Regional Government has the authority and is responsible for the Empowerment and Protection of Women.
2. The authority and responsibility for the Empowerment and Protection of Women as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. planning;
b. organizing;
c. implementation;
d. coordination;
e. budgeting;
f. supervision;
g. research and development;
h. standardization; and
i. management of development information in the Region.
3. The authority and responsibility for the Empowerment and Protection of Women as referred to in paragraph (2) is carried out in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. The implementation of the authority and responsibility of the Regional Government is carried out through coordination with other Regional Apparatus that have authority over fields in Women's Empowerment and Protection of Women.
5. The coordination mechanism as referred to in paragraph (4) is implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER III

WOMEN'S RIGHTS

Article 6

Every Woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue her lineage;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life in order to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose a partner in marriage in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 7

Women's Empowerment is directed to play a role and participate in the fields of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. economy;
d. social culture;
e. politics and government;
f. law;
g. employment;
h. social security; and
i. environmental conservation.

Article 10

Women's Empowerment in the Field of Economy is implemented through:
a. providing skills and job training;
b. facilitating the formation, strengthening, and development of productive economic and creative economy business groups;
c. facilitating capital; and
d. facilitating the development of marketing networks.

Article 11

Women's Empowerment in the field of social culture is implemented through:
a. increasing knowledge, attitudes and skills to encourage the fulfillment of tiered education according to potential to improve social status;
b. increasing awareness of various social problems in society through communication, information and education services;
c. increasing awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration; and
d. facilitating and efforts to preserve customs and develop science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women.

Article 12

Women's Empowerment in the field of politics and government is implemented through:
a. involving women in decision-making at various levels;
b. involving women in development planning at various levels;
c. encouraging increased involvement of women in organizations and providing opportunities for women to hold public positions;
d. facilitating women's participation in general elections;
e. self-development through organizations to associate, assemble and express opinions;
f. facilitating capacity building for prospective legislative candidates, political party members and/or women's community organizations in order to fulfill women's representation in legislative institutions of at least 30% (thirty percent);
g. facilitating increased women's political participation in legislative institutions;
h. facilitating facilities and infrastructure for women's community organizations or community organizations whose vision and mission is to fight for the fulfillment of women's rights in the Region;
i. conducting socialization in order to increase awareness of women's rights and women's participation in the field of politics; and
j. encouraging the formation of women's organizations and/or forums.

Article 16

Women's Empowerment in the field of environmental conservation is implemented through:
a. increasing the capacity of women, especially related to increasing environmental knowledge so that it can be highly beneficial for environmentally friendly development;
b. increasing environmental conservation education and environmental management in households;
c. increasing waste management education in the surrounding environment; and
d. increasing access for women to participate in environmental development.

Article 18

1. Implementation of Women's Protection through:
a. prevention of violence against women;
b. services for women victims of violence; and
c. strengthening Women's Protection institutions.
2. Implementation of Women's Protection as referred to in Article 18 may be provided to vulnerable women groups.
3. Vulnerable women groups as referred to in paragraph (2) include:
a. poor women;
b. women with disabilities;
c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. migrant women workers;
e. elderly women;
f. indigenous women;
g. women in disaster and social conflict situations;
h. women heads of households;
i. women informal workers;
j. women in situations of intolerance;
k. women victims/exposed to narcotics, psychotropics, and addictive substances;
l. women victims/exposed to terrorism, radicalism and extremism; and/or
m.other vulnerable women groups.

Article 19

1. Prevention of Violence Against Women as referred to in Article 18 letter a may be carried out in the following forms:
a. developing communication media, information, education and public campaigns;
b. developing educational materials and curricula;
c. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
d. establishing early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
e. providing consultation, information, education, and counseling services;
f. developing policies related to safe and friendly schools/madrasahs, boarding schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities;
g. establishing and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic boarding school students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders;
h. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media;
i. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
j. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;
k. increasing the capacity of regional governments, government agencies, institutions, community institutions, business sector, Islamic boarding schools, community organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities;
l. conducting cooperation with religious institutions, social community institutions, service institutions, universities, Islamic boarding schools, media, youth organizations, religious leaders and community leaders; and
m. conducting socialization of laws and regulations.
2. Forms of prevention of Violence Against Women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 20

Women victims of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to recovery;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain easy access to information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to social rehabilitation;
g. the right to prompt, appropriate, comfortable and needs-based complaint handling;
h. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process;
i. the right to assistance; and
j. the right to a sense of security.

Article 21

Services for women victims of violence may be carried out through:
a. receiving reports and/or outreach to victims of violence;
b. providing information about the rights of victims of violence;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, social rehabilitation, social empowerment, repatriation, and social reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for victims of violence and families of victims of violence that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating the needs of victims of violence with disabilities;
j. coordinating and cooperating on the fulfillment of the rights of victims of violence with other institutions; and
k. monitoring the fulfillment of the rights of victims of violence by law enforcement officials during the judicial process.

Article 22

1. Strengthening institutions for the Protection of Women may be carried out through capacity building, cooperation and integration with:
a. government agencies;
b. educational institutions;
c. religious institutions;
d. health service provider institutions;
e. professional institutions;
f. business sector;
g. service institutions;
h. Islamic boarding schools;
i. youth organizations;
j. legal aid organizations;
k. media;
l. religious leaders;
m. community leaders;
n. communities; and
o. families.
2. Strengthening and developing service provider institutions for the Protection of Women shall be implemented through:
a. capacity building of human resources of service provider institutions for the Protection of Women victims of violence at the Regional level;
b. provision of specific needs for women in emergency situations and special conditions at the Regional level;
c. development of communication, information, and education strategies on the protection of women's rights at the Regional level;
d. strengthening networks among service provider institutions for the Protection of Women at the Regional level;
e. facilitating efforts to fulfill the standards of service provider institutions for the Protection of Women at the Regional level;
f. developing service provider institutions for the Protection of Women at the Regional level.

Article 24

The mechanism for the implementation of the Empowerment and Protection of Women shall be carried out through the following stages:
a. formulation of policy planning for programs and activities;
b. budgeting of programs and activities;
c. implementation of programs and activities; and
d. monitoring, evaluation and reporting.

CHAPTER X

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 31

1. The Mayor through the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Protection shall conduct guidance and supervision over Women's Empowerment and Protection;
2. The form of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is as follows:
a. providing socialization to the community and business sector regarding Women's Empowerment and Protection policies;
b. providing educational media regarding Women's Empowerment and Protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community;
c. facilitating capacity building of institutions organizing Women's Empowerment and Protection;
d. facilitating forums for women's participation and women's organizations;
e. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, and international assistance for Women's Empowerment and Protection; and
f. providing awards to the community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have made efforts in Women's Empowerment and Protection.
3. The form of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities over Women's Empowerment and Protection.
4. The Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Empowerment and Protection shall report the results of guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (2) and paragraph (3) to the Mayor.

Article 15

Letter a
The criteria for social problems are having a life that is not humanly decent, which includes:
a. poverty;
b. abandonment;
c. disability;
d. remoteness;
e. social dysfunction and behavioral deviation;
f. disaster victims; and/or
g. victims of violence, exploitation, and discrimination.
